WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.267 line:92%
-Congratulations on your book

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
being the "New York Times"
best-seller.

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:04.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, you wrote about 1950s
Washington, D.C.

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:07.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a thriller.
This is about politicians.

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:10.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is about the swamp
and corruption.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.911 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would have thought
that if you wrote a book,

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:14.681 size:81% position:41%
you would maybe try to
escape from what you
talk about every day.

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:18.418
But it seemed like you took a --
you escaped via a cul-de-sac.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:20.520 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I went right back
into the swamp.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.088 line:92%
Yeah, no, it was exciting,

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:24.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I could write
about Washington today,

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:26.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a way,
by looking at it in 1954.

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:29.529
And the basic premise is
a young congressman and his wife

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.131 align:start size:94% position:6%
go down to Washington,
try to do good,

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:33.166 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they get swept away
in this conspiracy.

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:35.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
But what was fun was writing
about actual figures --

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:38.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
John F. Kennedy as a senator
and Joe McCarthy --

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.408
and putting them in the book,
having them be characters.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:43.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And have you gotten
good feedback from people

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
who, obviously, still live
in that world today?

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There's one Republican
congressional aide

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
who reached out to me and said
that it seemed like

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:52.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was trying to get
some things off my chest,

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:54.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I thought was funny
because --

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.222 size:75% position:38%
There is a lot of --
You know, they say

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
history doesn't repeat itself,
but it rhymes.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when you read
about Joe McCarthy

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you study about
Joe McCarthy,

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:03.663 line:92%
there's a lot of rhyming.

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:05.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-You hear a lot of rhyming.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
This guy
who was larger than life

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
and very popular
with the base of his party

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:10.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
and smeared a lot of people
and lied all the time,

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Washington
just didn't know what to do,

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.874
and reporters and members of the
Senate didn't know what to do.

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.310
-And we also look back --
I think there's a tendency

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
to look back on the '50s
as, like,

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:21.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
this really "apple pie" era
of America.

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there was -- It was --
Not maybe as much,

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it was rife with
corruption then, as well.

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:27.654 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. It was -- It was hard.
I mean, first of all,

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:30.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you're a white man,
it was probably pretty good.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:33.259
But for everybody who wasn't
a white man, it was awful.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:34.360 line:92%
But then, even beyond that,

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.330 align:start size:94% position:6%
the '50s, yeah,
in pop culture, it's idyllic.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:38.998 line:92%
It's serene. It's benign.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:42.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, like, in truth,
there was the Red Scare.

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.169 size:81% position:41% line:92%
There was McCarthyism.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
There were actual Communists
infiltrating government.

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was the atomic race.
It was horrible.

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.242
So, that's -- I thought
it would be fun to write.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.010 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because on the surface,
you know,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's all Sinatra
and Jack Kennedy,

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.314 line:92%
but, really, underneath it all,

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.149 line:92%
it's McCarthyism and real mass.

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:57.350 line:92%
-I want to ask you about this.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because, obviously,
you write a book.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:01.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
You go out, you do press
about your book.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:03.189 align:start size:94% position:6%
But because of
everything else you do,

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:05.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know I'm going to ask you
other questions.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:07.060 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm going to ask you
about your show on Sunday.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.429
I'm gonna ask you about
other people you've interviewed.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.297
Have you been surprised?
'Cause someone else who's doing

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
press right now is
President Bill Clinton.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.403
And he seems very caught
off guard when he is also asked

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:20.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
about specifically
the MeToo movement.

00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:22.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
Have you been surprised...
-Stunned.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:22.842 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-...at his failure

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.611 size:81% position:41%
to predict that those
questions were coming?

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:28.548
-First of all, I know some
of the people on that team.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:32.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm sure that
they tried to prepare him,

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I've been amazed
at how poorly

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:36.222 line:92%
he's handled these questions.

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:39.392 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, a lot of them,
you could have anticipated.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:42.395 size:81% position:41%
The world has changed
in just the last year.

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:45.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was, I mean, I think --
Who was the Emmy --

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.667 align:start size:94% position:6%
Who was the Tonys host
a year ago?

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:48.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Kevin Spacey.
-Kevin Spacey.

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:50.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-I mean, that was a year ago.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And I thought
he should have done it again.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:55.241 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.610 size:84% position:42% line:92%
I thought he was great.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.511 line:92%
-So, I mean, it's just --

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:01.681 size:81% position:41%
The world has changed,
so you have to adapt.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:05.251
And I would think he would have
had automatic answers for,

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Do you owe Monica Lewinsky
an apology?"

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.087 size:75% position:38%
"What do you make of
the MeToo movement?"

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:11.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
"What do you make of this
individual case, Harvey
Weinstein, whoever?"

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, boy, some of the answers
have been just --

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
You couldn't --
If you wanted to write

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:19.432
the worst answer he could give,
it as almost as if --

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:20.600 size:78% position:39% line:92%
That one he did today

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.770 size:78% position:39%
about whether or not
you can grope people.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. It's a --
-People have different rules

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
today about where
you're allowed to touch them

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.276 line:92%
on their bodies involuntarily.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:33.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was -- Yeah.
It was not well thought-out.

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:35.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And then he said --
And then he ended it with,

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
"But, you know,
maybe I'm old-fashioned."

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Not a good way
to frame it, yeah.

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:42.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
I do want to ask about someone
else who had a real moment

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:44.591 size:78% position:39% line:92%
in the MeToo movement

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:47.794 align:start size:94% position:6%
and was so tragic to lose,
was Anthony Bourdain.

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:49.529
-Yeah.
-Who was your colleague at CNN.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:51.264 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-I found it heartbreaking.

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:54.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
Did you know him well?
-I knew him. I knew him okay.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:57.971
I wasn't one of his closest
friends, but I knew him okay.

00:03:57.971 --> 00:03:59.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, in fact, it was through
the MeToo movement

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:03.343
that he and I started talking a
lot because he was so outraged.

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:05.511
And we had done a lot of
coverage about Harvey Weinstein

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:08.881
last year, and he would reach
out, and we would talk a lot.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.850
And that's one of the things --
It's such a loss

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.454
for people who were inspired
by him for any number of ways.

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:16.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
His show was about
the humanity of us all.

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:18.258 size:78% position:39%
-It really was, yeah.
-But he really had --

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:21.761
The side that I saw of him
that I loved was his passion,

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:26.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
his righteous indignation
against injustice

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:27.767 line:92%
and against all the people

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:30.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
who let Harvey Weinstein
get away with it for so long.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not like
there are a ton of people

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:34.340 align:start size:94% position:6%
who have that
righteous indignation,

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
so the world is much poorer
for his having left.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.077 line:92%
-Yeah. He will really be missed.

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:40.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much for
sharing that and being here.

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:43.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's always such a pleasure.
Congrats on the book.

